The '''Kafto-Ethiopian Charter''' ({{lang-bl|La Carta Kafto-Etíopes}}; {{Langx|kz|ߌߥߋ ߊߘߋߤߎ߲ ߞߊߝߙߊߥߎߞߏ-ߌߕߌߦߏߔߌߊ|Ìwé Àdéhùn Kàfáùkò-Etiópíà}}; {{Langx|mao|Karta Kafto-Etiope|}}) comprises the agreements signed on 25 June 1793 by representatives of the [[Sanyan|State of Sanyan]] and the [[Maroon Confederacy]], which had been cooperating to undermine the [[Ethiopian slave trade]] since the appointment of Oba [[Nsuukidem of Sanyan|Nsuukidem]] in 1652. Nsuukidem, supported by his council, and through his ties to figures within the [[Baliscan archipelago|archipelago]], financed the [[Maroon pirates]] to halt, intercept and liberate Illypnian slave ships on their way to the [[Algarbes]]. This was already a common practice of the Maroon pirates dating back to the founding of [[Daramazán]], however as shown by historical records, pirate attacks intensified in the years after 1653.

Between the outbreak of the [[Maroon Wars|first slave revolts]] in 1593, largely in [[Bayara]], and the [[Volisanian Empire]] attempt at reconquering the archipelago (the [[Baliscan War of Independence]]) in 1808, more than 375,000 formerly enslaved and free-born black people who faced social and legal oppression in the [[Ethiopian world]], had been liberated. Eight Volisanian  expeditions invaded the Maroon territory in an attempt to conquer it between 1699 and 1804, but these were all beaten back with considerable loss of life as a result of violent guerrilla tactics. The Confederacy later intervened in the [[Baliscan Civil War]], playing a decisive role in beating back the [[Siege of Castejón]], and in subsequent years developed an amiable relationship with [[Anyos Federales|Reconstructionist Balisca]]. The Confederacy continued to exercise sovereignty until it confederated with the [[Balisca|Baliscan Confederation]] at the [[1883 Confederal Convention]], from which the Balisca assumed the obligations of the Charter.
